1. WHAT IS JAVA?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Java is a high-level, object-oriented, class-based, and
platform-independent programming language developed by
James Gosling at Sun Microsystems in 1995.

It follows the principle of "Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)"
because Java source code is compiled into bytecode, which is
executed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).

Java is widely used for developing enterprise applications,
web applications, Android applications, desktop software,
and backend systems.


======================================================================

2. ADVANTAGES OF JAVA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Java provides several advantages:

• Platform Independent (WORA)
• Object-Oriented
• Simple
• Secure
• Robust
• Portable
• Multithreading Support
• High Performance (JIT Compiler)
• Distributed
• Rich Standard Library

These features make Java suitable for enterprise, banking,
finance, web, and backend applications.


======================================================================

3. WHY IS JAVA PLATFORM INDEPENDENT?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Java is platform independent because it is compiled into
platform-independent bytecode instead of machine code.

The Java compiler converts source code (.java) into bytecode
(.class).

The JVM available on each operating system converts this
bytecode into machine code and executes it.

Since only the JVM changes for different operating systems,
the same bytecode runs everywhere.

This is called:

Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)


======================================================================

4. WHAT IS THE ENTRY POINT OF A JAVA PROGRAM?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

The entry point of a Java application is the main() method.

When the JVM starts a Java application, it searches for
the following method:

public static void main(String[] args)

Execution always begins from this method.


======================================================================

5. EXPLAIN
   public static void main(String[] args)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

public

• Access Modifier
• Accessible from anywhere
• JVM can invoke it

--------------------------------------------------

static

• Belongs to the class
• JVM can call it without creating an object

--------------------------------------------------

void

• Return type
• Returns nothing

--------------------------------------------------

main

• Predefined method name
• JVM searches for this method first

--------------------------------------------------

String[] args

• Array of String objects
• Stores command-line arguments


======================================================================

6. CAN main() METHOD BE OVERLOADED?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Yes.

The main() method can be overloaded like any other method.

However,

JVM always calls

public static void main(String[] args)

The overloaded versions must be called manually.


Example

public static void main(String[] args)

public static void main(int x)


======================================================================

7. CAN THE main() METHOD SIGNATURE BE CHANGED?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

No.

The JVM looks for the exact signature

public static void main(String[] args)

If the signature is changed,
the JVM cannot identify it as the entry point,
and the program will not start.

Important:

String[] args

and

String args[]

Both are valid.


