1. WHAT IS INHERITANCE?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Inheritance is one of the four pillars of Object-Oriented
Programming.

It is a mechanism through which a child class acquires the
accessible properties and methods of a parent class.

In Java, class inheritance is achieved using the extends keyword.

Main benefit:

• Code Reusability

It also establishes an "IS-A" relationship between classes.

Example:

class Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle starts");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
}

Here:

Vehicle → Parent Class
Car     → Child Class

Car can use the accessible methods of Vehicle.


======================================================================
2. TYPES OF INHERITANCE IN JAVA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Java supports the following inheritance structures:

1. Single Inheritance
2. Multilevel Inheritance
3. Hierarchical Inheritance

Multiple and Hybrid inheritance are NOT supported through
classes, but similar behavior can be achieved using interfaces.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. SINGLE INHERITANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

One child class inherits from one parent class.

        Parent
           │
           ▼
         Child


Example:

class Animal {
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}


----------------------------------------------------------------------
2. MULTILEVEL INHERITANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A class inherits from another class, which itself inherits
from another class.

      Grandparent
           │
           ▼
         Parent
           │
           ▼
          Child


Example:

class Grandparent {
}

class Parent extends Grandparent {
}

class Child extends Parent {
}


----------------------------------------------------------------------
3. HIERARCHICAL INHERITANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class.

             Parent
             /    \
            /      \
        Child1    Child2


Example:

class Animal {
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}

class Cat extends Animal {
}


----------------------------------------------------------------------
4. MULTIPLE INHERITANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

One child class inherits from multiple parent classes.

        Parent1      Parent2
             \        /
              \      /
               Child


Java does NOT support multiple inheritance through classes.

Reason:

Ambiguity / Diamond Problem


However, similar behavior can be achieved using interfaces.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
5. HYBRID INHERITANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Combination of two or more types of inheritance.

Java does NOT support hybrid inheritance through classes.

It can be achieved using interfaces.


======================================================================
3. WHY DOESN'T JAVA SUPPORT MULTIPLE INHERITANCE THROUGH CLASSES?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes
because it can create ambiguity when two parent classes contain
methods with the same signature.

Example:

        Parent1          Parent2
           │                │
           │ same method    │ same method
           └───────┬────────┘
                   │
                 Child

If Child inherits from both Parent1 and Parent2,
the compiler/JVM would have ambiguity about which
method implementation should be used.

This is commonly known as the:

DIAMOND PROBLEM

Java avoids this ambiguity by not allowing multiple
inheritance through classes.

Multiple inheritance of behavior can be achieved using
interfaces.


======================================================================
