======================================================================
1. WHAT IS A PACKAGE IN JAVA?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

A package in Java is a mechanism used to group related classes,
interfaces, and other Java types together.

Packages help:

• Organize code
• Avoid naming conflicts
• Provide access control
• Improve maintainability
• Support code reuse


Example:

package com.example.student;

public class Student {

    // class implementation
}

Here:

com.example.student
        ↓
Package

Student
        ↓
Class inside the package


======================================================================
2. WHY DO WE USE PACKAGES?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Main purposes of packages:

1. Code Organization
   → Groups related classes/interfaces together.

2. Avoid Naming Conflicts
   → Classes with the same name can exist in different packages.

3. Access Control
   → Package-private members can be accessed only within
     the same package.

4. Maintainability
   → Makes large applications easier to manage.

5. Reusability
   → Classes from a package can be imported and reused.


Example:

import calculator.Calculator;

Calculator c = new Calculator();

IMPORTANT:

Importing a class ≠ inheritance.

`import` is used to access/use a class from another package.

`extends` is used for inheritance.


======================================================================
3. WHAT ARE ACCESS MODIFIERS?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Access modifiers in Java define the visibility and accessibility
of classes, methods, variables, and constructors.

They control where a member can be accessed from.

Java provides four access levels:

1. public
2. protected
3. default / package-private
4. private


======================================================================
4. PUBLIC
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

A `public` member can be accessed from anywhere, provided the
class itself is accessible.

Example:

public class Calculator {

    public void add() {
        System.out.println("Addition");
    }
}

The public method can be accessed from other classes and packages.


Scope:

Same Class       → YES
Same Package     → YES
Subclass         → YES
Other Package    → YES


Memory Trick:

public → EVERYWHERE


======================================================================
5. PRIVATE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

A `private` member can be accessed only within the same class.

Example:

class Bank {

    private double balance;

    private void calculateInterest() {
        // implementation
    }
}

The private members cannot be directly accessed outside `Bank`.

Scope:

Same Class       → YES
Same Package     → NO
Subclass         → NO
Other Package    → NO


Memory Trick:

private → SAME CLASS ONLY


======================================================================
6. PROTECTED
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

A `protected` member can be accessed:

• Within the same package
• By subclasses outside the package through inheritance

Example:

class Animal {

    protected void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void display() {
        sound();
    }
}


Scope:

Same Class       → YES
Same Package     → YES
Subclass         → YES*
Other Package    → NO


*Subclass access outside the package is subject to Java's
protected inheritance rules.


Memory Trick:

protected → PACKAGE + SUBCLASS


======================================================================
7. DEFAULT / PACKAGE-PRIVATE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

If no access modifier is specified, Java gives the member
default/package-private access.

It can be accessed only within the same package.

Example:

class Student {

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Student");
    }
}

Here `display()` has default/package-private access.

Scope:

Same Class       → YES
Same Package     → YES
Subclass         → NO*
Other Package    → NO


*An ordinary subclass in another package cannot access it merely
because it is a subclass.


Memory Trick:

default → SAME PACKAGE ONLY


======================================================================
8. ACCESS MODIFIER COMPARISON
----------------------------------------------------------------------

+------------+-------+---------+-----------+----------------+
| Modifier   | Class | Package | Subclass  | Other Package |
+------------+-------+---------+-----------+----------------+
| public     |  YES  |   YES   |    YES    |      YES       |
| protected  |  YES  |   YES   |    YES*   |      NO        |
| default    |  YES  |   YES   |    NO*    |      NO        |
| private    |  YES  |   NO    |    NO     |      NO        |
+------------+-------+---------+-----------+----------------+

*Protected has special inheritance rules for subclasses outside
the package.


======================================================================
9. ACCESS MODIFIERS – QUICK VISUAL
----------------------------------------------------------------------

public
   ↓
Everywhere

protected
   ↓
Same Package + Subclasses

default
   ↓
Same Package

private
   ↓
Same Class


======================================================================
COMMON INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

❌ "Default is the same as private."

✅ No.

Default → same package
Private → same class only


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Protected can be accessed everywhere by subclasses."

✅ Not exactly.

A subclass in another package can access protected members through
inheritance, subject to Java's protected-access rules.


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Import means inheritance."

✅ No.

import
→ Allows using a class/interface from another package.

extends
→ Inheritance.

implements
→ Interface implementation.


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Private members are accessible from child classes."

✅ No.

Private members are not directly accessible in subclasses.


